  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 113 PM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nOn Friday, an area of subtropical moisture is forecast to move north-\nnortheast from old Mexico and move towards far southeast Colorado. \nThis area of moisture, if it verifies, could bring a period or \nprecip to far southeast Colorado on Friday, However, I have seen \nsituations similar to this one in the past, with the moisture \nmoving too far east and moving into KS. For now my confidence is not \nhigh on this forecast verifying. \n\nIn the meantime, moisture will increase over the higher terrain, and \nwe will likely see some isolated to scattered showers in the \nmountains on Friday.\n\nFor Saturday, weak mid level ridging moves over the region. Any \nshowers which develop this day will be isolated, and mainly confined \nto the higher terrain.\n\nThe weather ramps up Sunday and is fcst to last into Tuesday. A \nSharp mid level trough is fcst to drop southeast from the Pacific \nnorthwest. As this trough move through, another band of subtropical \nmoisture coming off the California coast moves across the region. At \nthis time this looks to be a mountain storm. Temps will be warm \ninitially, but will cool off quite a bit as the mid level trough \nmoves towards the region. Overall, we would see some rain at higher \nelevations initially, but believe the brunt of the precip in the \nmtns will be snow. This is shown in the NBM 1D forecast statistical \nproducts for the higher elevation points to our west. \n\nWith that said, if the storm does wrap up considerably as it moves \ninto KS, we could get some wrap-around moisture over the plains, \nespecially during the Monday/Tuesday time frame. If the system drops \na bit farther south and east as hinted by the latest 12Z EC, then \nthe plains may see a more favorable upslope pattern develop. \n\nHow this system impacts us will be very dependent on the track and \nintensification of the trough. Usually storms dropping southeast and \nintensifying as they do, can bring impactful weather to the region. \n\nOverall, we should see storm system which brings beneficial snow \npack to the the mountains Sunday-TUesday. Of interest, per USGS, \nLake Powell has risen 14 feet since mid September.",
